WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.678 --> 00:00:05.027
- I don't like mindless
content. I've never liked it.

00:00:05.027 --> 00:00:07.814
I like to think about what I'm watching,

00:00:07.814 --> 00:00:09.477
what I'm taking in, what I'm listening to.

00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:11.453
Yeah, humor's always been an
important part of my life.

00:00:11.453 --> 00:00:14.083
Like, since, like, middle school.

00:00:14.083 --> 00:00:15.906
- Hello, Believe Nation, it's Evan.

00:00:15.906 --> 00:00:18.676
My one word is believe
and I believe in people

00:00:18.676 --> 00:00:20.530
more than they believe in themselves.

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:23.442
And my sincere hope is
that if you see in yourself

00:00:23.442 --> 00:00:26.893
what I see you, you'll be
able to change the planet.

00:00:26.893 --> 00:00:29.048
So to help you on your journey,
today, we're going to learn

00:00:29.048 --> 00:00:32.238
from stand-up comedian and
actor, Jerrod Carmichael.

00:00:32.238 --> 00:00:33.536
It's a good last name.

00:00:33.536 --> 00:00:36.374
And my take on his top
10 rules for success.

00:00:36.374 --> 00:00:38.188
Rule number one is my
personal favorite, and

00:00:38.188 --> 00:00:40.344
I'd love to know which one
you guys like the best.

00:00:40.344 --> 00:00:42.529
Also, as you're watching, if
you hear something that really

00:00:42.529 --> 00:00:44.313
resonates with you, please
leave it down in the

00:00:44.313 --> 00:00:46.192
comments below and put
quotes around it so other

00:00:46.192 --> 00:00:48.248
people can be inspired by it as well.

00:00:48.248 --> 00:00:50.060
And if you leave it within
the first couple hours

00:00:50.060 --> 00:00:52.225
of this video going live,
you have a chance to win one

00:00:52.225 --> 00:00:54.068
of two daily prizes.

00:00:54.068 --> 00:00:56.151
(swoosh)

00:00:57.355 --> 00:00:59.938
(upbeat music)

00:01:06.105 --> 00:01:07.292
(swoosh)

00:01:07.292 --> 00:01:12.025
- I don't like mindless
content. I've never liked it.

00:01:12.025 --> 00:01:14.813
I like to think about what I'm watching,

00:01:14.813 --> 00:01:16.558
what I'm taking in, what I'm listening to.

00:01:16.558 --> 00:01:20.119
So, even music, I can't
listen to music that

00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:23.547
doesn't challenge me in
anyway and I never could.

00:01:23.547 --> 00:01:25.696
It's just kind of who I am as a person.

00:01:25.696 --> 00:01:28.761
It was, I guess, embedded
in my DNA to just, really

00:01:28.761 --> 00:01:32.311
want to feel something,
figure something out.

00:01:32.311 --> 00:01:36.478
And not just tell jokes for
the sake of telling jokes.

00:01:37.540 --> 00:01:41.947
I like to say things that,
at least, can be meaningful.

00:01:41.947 --> 00:01:42.780
And ...

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.245
People ...

00:01:45.245 --> 00:01:48.929
I know that I'm doing my
job when I see someone react

00:01:48.929 --> 00:01:51.611
other than just a laugh,
actually give me a reaction.

00:01:51.611 --> 00:01:54.089
And it's not always just
turning as much as it's just

00:01:54.089 --> 00:01:57.464
like, "Oh no" like,
people shaking their head.

00:01:57.464 --> 00:02:00.608
Comedy, a lot of times, has
become this court jester type

00:02:00.608 --> 00:02:04.775
thing where everyone just goes
says the craziest, brashest,

00:02:05.742 --> 00:02:09.710
most outlandish absurd silly
thing and it means nothing.

00:02:09.710 --> 00:02:13.444
And I don't want to say things
that don't mean anything.

00:02:13.444 --> 00:02:15.309
- So you like sort of
putting the audience in a

00:02:15.309 --> 00:02:16.835
scenario where they could turn on you.

00:02:16.835 --> 00:02:17.668
And then it's a

00:02:17.668 --> 00:02:18.501
- Yeah.

00:02:18.501 --> 00:02:20.185
- bit more work for you as the artist

00:02:20.185 --> 00:02:21.576
to kind of make sure that they don't.

00:02:21.576 --> 00:02:25.218
- Yeah. Yeah. It's more
work, it's more exciting,

00:02:25.218 --> 00:02:26.736
and it' more fulfilling.

00:02:26.736 --> 00:02:29.083
- When did you notice that I can do this

00:02:29.083 --> 00:02:32.712
and I can turn a phrase
or make people laugh at

00:02:32.712 --> 00:02:34.698
something that they didn't see before?

00:02:34.698 --> 00:02:37.302
- 8th grade was really big.

00:02:37.302 --> 00:02:38.135
- Oh yeah?

00:02:38.135 --> 00:02:42.057
- Yeah, it was an important time for me.

00:02:42.057 --> 00:02:44.652
Just having this teacher
that encouraged me

00:02:44.652 --> 00:02:47.422
to read a lot independently.

00:02:47.422 --> 00:02:51.372
And to question everything,
gather information.

00:02:51.372 --> 00:02:54.988
I remember one time, he started
doing these class debates

00:02:54.988 --> 00:02:57.431
and there was this article.

00:02:57.431 --> 00:03:01.903
I don't remember exactly what
was in the article but it was

00:03:01.903 --> 00:03:04.586
about a man that committed a crime

00:03:04.586 --> 00:03:06.516
but he had a somewhat
righteous cause for doing it.

00:03:06.516 --> 00:03:07.474
I remember those details

00:03:07.474 --> 00:03:08.524
- Okay.

00:03:08.524 --> 00:03:09.907
- But, he was dividing up the class and

00:03:09.907 --> 00:03:12.840
who agreed with this guy
and thought that he was

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:15.508
justified in doing it and who disagreed.

00:03:15.508 --> 00:03:17.927
I remember he said "who
thinks he wasn't justified?"

00:03:17.927 --> 00:03:20.390
And the entire class except
me raised their hand.

00:03:20.390 --> 00:03:21.223
- Really?

00:03:21.223 --> 00:03:23.177
- And then you know, like,
"well, who agrees with the guy?"

00:03:23.177 --> 00:03:27.193
And I raised my hand and then
we argued for like 25 minutes.

00:03:27.193 --> 00:03:30.225
I remember it being the
most fun time I'd ever

00:03:30.225 --> 00:03:33.121
had in class, ever had in school.

00:03:33.121 --> 00:03:35.337
It meant the most to
me and I just remember

00:03:35.337 --> 00:03:39.348
holding my own against 20 other students.

00:03:39.348 --> 00:03:42.491
- That's sounds strangely
akin to stand up because

00:03:42.491 --> 00:03:44.996
it's one guy with the whole sort of

00:03:44.996 --> 00:03:46.941
audience that you have
to sort of win over.

00:03:46.941 --> 00:03:50.273
- Yeah, exactly and I
think that one experience,

00:03:50.273 --> 00:03:53.643
that single experience, it formed

00:03:53.643 --> 00:03:56.060
so much of my life for humor.

00:03:57.607 --> 00:04:01.400
More than any individual
experience I can think of.

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:03.758
- It must have also been
notable that you were

00:04:03.758 --> 00:04:06.237
the only person that took
an opposing view point.

00:04:06.237 --> 00:04:07.542
- Yeah, and it was natural.

00:04:07.542 --> 00:04:10.463
And that's kind of a rule
for stand-up for me too

00:04:10.463 --> 00:04:14.415
is that never be
contrarian for the sake of.

00:04:14.415 --> 00:04:15.714
- Right.

00:04:15.714 --> 00:04:20.509
- Always have a truthful
intention behind what you say.

00:04:20.509 --> 00:04:23.114
Because that's what
people will connect with.

00:04:23.114 --> 00:04:27.790
That's what will give license
to people's attention.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.129
People will actually give you
their time if they know that

00:04:31.129 --> 00:04:33.771
it's coming from an honest
place and that it's really

00:04:33.771 --> 00:04:36.713
a thought or emotion that
you want to work out.

00:04:36.713 --> 00:04:39.657
I think that people respond to that.

00:04:39.657 --> 00:04:44.636
- When did you know that this
is what your calling was?

00:04:44.636 --> 00:04:47.562
- When I was depressed
working at a shoe store.

00:04:47.562 --> 00:04:49.416
I was like, you know, I got
to do something else. (laughs)

00:04:49.416 --> 00:04:51.511
- [Dan] Were you a funny salesman?

00:04:51.511 --> 00:04:53.851
- Yeah. Humor's always been
an important part of my life.

00:04:53.851 --> 00:04:55.895
Since, like, middle school.

00:04:55.895 --> 00:04:57.401
- [Dan] But you were selling tennis shoes?

00:04:57.401 --> 00:04:59.920
- Selling tennis shoes. But
you use it for anything.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:02.554
You use it for whatever you're working on.

00:05:02.554 --> 00:05:03.981
- [Dan Patrick] You
meet women in that way?

00:05:03.981 --> 00:05:06.474
- Yeah. That was my way in.

00:05:06.474 --> 00:05:07.440
- [Dan] Yeah.

00:05:07.440 --> 00:05:08.273
- Yeah.

00:05:08.273 --> 00:05:09.106
- [Dan] Through their feet?

00:05:09.106 --> 00:05:11.033
- Yeah, through their feet. (laughs)

00:05:11.033 --> 00:05:12.891
I never really met
girls at the shoe store.

00:05:12.891 --> 00:05:13.920
You meet 'em at, a, ...

00:05:13.920 --> 00:05:14.779
- [Dan] Oh, okay.

00:05:14.779 --> 00:05:18.805
everywhere else but humor
was the most important thing.

00:05:18.805 --> 00:05:20.424
- You talk about gentrification and

00:05:20.424 --> 00:05:23.199
how it's affecting folks.

00:05:23.199 --> 00:05:26.890
It's not that it's funny,
it's that it's real

00:05:26.890 --> 00:05:28.121
that makes us laugh.

00:05:28.121 --> 00:05:28.954
- Yeah.

00:05:28.954 --> 00:05:29.787
- You know what I mean?

00:05:29.787 --> 00:05:30.620
- Yeah.

00:05:30.620 --> 00:05:32.509
- And that's the kind of
smart, intelligent comedy

00:05:32.509 --> 00:05:34.654
that I feel like you guys
are writing on this show

00:05:34.654 --> 00:05:38.873
that you're touching on real
ass issues while laughing

00:05:38.873 --> 00:05:40.383
because we're laughing at ourselves.

00:05:40.383 --> 00:05:42.390
- People connect with
truth more than anything.

00:05:42.390 --> 00:05:43.223
- Yeah.

00:05:43.223 --> 00:05:46.248
- Especially, I mean,
we live in a time where

00:05:46.248 --> 00:05:49.255
we were just talking about it
there's so many reality shows

00:05:49.255 --> 00:05:52.602
and so many things to
where people see reality

00:05:52.602 --> 00:05:55.419
and they live in it in
their entertainment so much

00:05:55.419 --> 00:05:58.787
that if you turn on a sitcom
and people are just saying

00:05:58.787 --> 00:06:01.148
these crazy things that
you know isn't real life

00:06:01.148 --> 00:06:03.891
then you have nothing to truly respond to.

00:06:03.891 --> 00:06:06.788
- Yo, that's why I can't
watch a lot of the old sitcoms

00:06:06.788 --> 00:06:08.773
unless I'm just trying
to escape and go dumb.

00:06:08.773 --> 00:06:09.606
- Yeah.

00:06:09.606 --> 00:06:10.439
- [Female] Ha.

00:06:10.439 --> 00:06:11.272
- Right?

00:06:11.272 --> 00:06:12.105
- [Female] Wow.

00:06:12.105 --> 00:06:12.938
- I don't want people ...

00:06:12.938 --> 00:06:17.522
With anything, I never want
people to turn their minds off.

00:06:17.522 --> 00:06:18.709
- It's like all of the art that I love,

00:06:18.709 --> 00:06:20.990
my favorite artists, my
favorite rappers, my favorites

00:06:20.990 --> 00:06:22.939
make me think and make
me question things and

00:06:22.939 --> 00:06:24.958
I have to listen again
and I have to watch again.

00:06:24.958 --> 00:06:25.945
- [Sway] Mm-hmm.

00:06:25.945 --> 00:06:27.286
- And so, I just want
to contribute to that.

00:06:27.286 --> 00:06:28.477
- [Sway] Mm-hmm.

00:06:28.477 --> 00:06:31.411
- With the show and with
stand-up or anything.

00:06:31.411 --> 00:06:34.873
- Did exploring apathy or
at least sort of your fear

00:06:34.873 --> 00:06:37.675
of not feeling enough or the
feelings about not feeling

00:06:37.675 --> 00:06:41.830
in this special shot just a
few days after the election

00:06:41.830 --> 00:06:43.366
did it feel kind of dangerous or

00:06:43.366 --> 00:06:44.663
like you were walking a thin line?

00:06:44.663 --> 00:06:46.697
Cause I will say when I was watching it,

00:06:46.697 --> 00:06:49.231
there was something about
like that was one of the most

00:06:49.231 --> 00:06:51.086
dangerous things that I felt I had seen a

00:06:51.086 --> 00:06:52.470
comedian do in a long time.

00:06:52.470 --> 00:06:53.327
- (laughs) yeah.

00:06:53.327 --> 00:06:54.407
- And it wouldn't have been that dangerous

00:06:54.407 --> 00:06:55.240
- Yeah.

00:06:55.240 --> 00:06:56.446
- A year ago. But there
was something about you

00:06:56.446 --> 00:06:59.456
walking that rope and trying
to make this approachable

00:06:59.456 --> 00:07:01.448
for people and make them relate to you and

00:07:01.448 --> 00:07:03.453
understand you and not turn on you.

00:07:03.453 --> 00:07:05.262
- Yeah. Which is the fun part of it.

00:07:05.262 --> 00:07:06.128
- Yeah.

00:07:06.128 --> 00:07:07.259
- That's where it's fun.

00:07:07.259 --> 00:07:10.889
You know, I mean, that's
where comedy is it's most fun.

00:07:10.889 --> 00:07:14.837
And I think can be at it's best where it's

00:07:14.837 --> 00:07:18.788
someone exploring an unpopular
thought or emotion on stage

00:07:18.788 --> 00:07:23.242
and I like to treat audience
members like the adults that

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:26.667
they are and we can have a
discussion and you can completely

00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:30.757
disagree with me you can feel
the opposite of how I feel

00:07:30.757 --> 00:07:34.114
and still listen and still
understand and still ...

00:07:34.114 --> 00:07:38.362
My job is to articulate my
point and I just try and

00:07:38.362 --> 00:07:41.370
articulate my point and
hopefully you laugh at the end

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:42.706
or at least feel something.

00:07:42.706 --> 00:07:44.681
- Do you think that there's a line that

00:07:44.681 --> 00:07:46.176
people should or shouldn't cross?

00:07:46.176 --> 00:07:48.054
Or do you think that that
line itself is it just like

00:07:48.054 --> 00:07:50.658
well, is it funny and do you
have an original thought?

00:07:50.658 --> 00:07:52.758
And if you don't then there's
probably a line for you.

00:07:52.758 --> 00:07:55.258
- You should have a intention.

00:07:56.747 --> 00:08:00.863
You know. The line is
where your intention is.

00:08:00.863 --> 00:08:04.735
If your intention is
to just say some crazy

00:08:04.735 --> 00:08:07.101
into a microphone, then maybe you

00:08:07.101 --> 00:08:08.837
shouldn't have a microphone.

00:08:08.837 --> 00:08:11.552
Like I'm just going to say the
most absurd thing in the world

00:08:11.552 --> 00:08:14.594
and you're going to get mad
and whatev ... like, no.

00:08:14.594 --> 00:08:18.458
But if you're intention
is to explore a thought,

00:08:18.458 --> 00:08:20.033
then there is no line.

00:08:20.033 --> 00:08:23.325
- How did you develop your stand-up act?

00:08:23.325 --> 00:08:24.873
- Just kind of doing it. I just jumped in.

00:08:24.873 --> 00:08:27.223
I didn't ... I'm from North Carolina

00:08:27.223 --> 00:08:29.529
but I started in L.A. cause I
just wanted, I'm competitive.

00:08:29.529 --> 00:08:30.537
- [Dan] Did you go to the comedy store?

00:08:30.537 --> 00:08:31.370
- I'm competitive in that sense.

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:32.203
- [Dan] Oh, you are?

00:08:32.203 --> 00:08:34.967
- That's where the heart
of an athlete (laughs)

00:08:34.967 --> 00:08:37.299
is where you're just wanting to

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:38.548
- [Dan] So you're the Jordan of comedy

00:08:38.548 --> 00:08:39.495
is what you're saying?

00:08:39.495 --> 00:08:40.328
- God bless you.

00:08:40.328 --> 00:08:41.161
- [Dan] Yes.

00:08:41.161 --> 00:08:42.831
(laughs)

00:08:42.831 --> 00:08:47.244
- NBC is in a place of
redevelopment which is kind of why I

00:08:47.244 --> 00:08:49.975
was attracted to NBC in the first place.

00:08:49.975 --> 00:08:53.027
I only pitched to NBC cause I was only

00:08:53.027 --> 00:08:55.262
going to sell it to NBC.

00:08:55.262 --> 00:08:56.095
- Really?

00:08:56.095 --> 00:08:56.928
- Because I was making a show for NBC.

00:08:56.928 --> 00:08:57.761
- Wow

00:08:57.761 --> 00:08:58.594
- And you know and partly-

00:08:58.594 --> 00:08:59.470
- Usually you pitch to 10 people.

00:08:59.470 --> 00:09:04.275
- Yeah, yeah and I did you know
I did a deal with Universal

00:09:04.275 --> 00:09:08.800
because, yeah, they can
pitch to other places but

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:10.573
I knew NBC would be first
and that's the only place

00:09:10.573 --> 00:09:12.352
I was going to do the show. That was it.

00:09:12.352 --> 00:09:15.085
- I understand the show
was a realization of

00:09:15.085 --> 00:09:16.952
a life long dream of yours?

00:09:16.952 --> 00:09:20.797
- I always wanted a show on NBC.
That was kind of of a, yeah.

00:09:20.797 --> 00:09:22.595
- Starting at what age?

00:09:22.595 --> 00:09:24.228
- I think I was like, 11 or 12.

00:09:24.228 --> 00:09:26.857
I used to tell my brother
and my family I wanted a show

00:09:26.857 --> 00:09:30.841
on NBC because a lot of my
favorite shows were on there.

00:09:30.841 --> 00:09:33.413
Everything from, I watched
television with my parents

00:09:33.413 --> 00:09:37.759
so I watched Cheers and The
Cosby Show and you watch,

00:09:37.759 --> 00:09:40.812
you know A Different World
and you watch Seinfeld

00:09:40.812 --> 00:09:43.626
and we watched all these
things and I was like,

00:09:43.626 --> 00:09:44.459
well I want to be on that.

00:09:44.459 --> 00:09:46.224
Fresh prince is on that, you know.

00:09:46.224 --> 00:09:48.468
And that was kind of the goal.

00:09:48.468 --> 00:09:50.884
I wanted Thursday nights
at eight but, you know,

00:09:50.884 --> 00:09:53.478
Wednesdays at nine is fine. You know.

00:09:53.478 --> 00:09:54.922
You can't have it all, Larry.

00:09:54.922 --> 00:09:58.138
- When you're making a decision
to discuss a specific issue,

00:09:58.138 --> 00:10:01.493
social or political or whatever
it may be, what's going on?

00:10:01.493 --> 00:10:03.572
Is it, are you building
a story around that issue

00:10:03.572 --> 00:10:06.377
or does the issue happen
authentically through

00:10:06.377 --> 00:10:09.563
the story you wanted
to tell to begin with?

00:10:09.563 --> 00:10:12.063
- A lot of times it's based on

00:10:13.362 --> 00:10:15.279
discussions, arguments,

00:10:16.190 --> 00:10:20.279
topics, that I talk about with my friends,

00:10:20.279 --> 00:10:22.906
that I talk about with my family.

00:10:22.906 --> 00:10:25.681
Then we just explore it honestly.

00:10:25.681 --> 00:10:29.265
And ask questions and explore
curiosity and that's it.

00:10:29.265 --> 00:10:32.522
Never the illusion of edge of, you know,

00:10:32.522 --> 00:10:35.481
cause if you're going to
pick these topics it's like,

00:10:35.481 --> 00:10:39.538
well I like speaking to
adults and I have adult

00:10:39.538 --> 00:10:43.225
conversations, like the best
version that I know how to have

00:10:43.225 --> 00:10:44.770
given the format.

00:10:44.770 --> 00:10:49.334
And again just being as real
as possible like not ...

00:10:49.334 --> 00:10:51.617
There isn't a message.

00:10:51.617 --> 00:10:53.075
There's no message.

00:10:53.075 --> 00:10:56.210
I'm not trying to get, I'm
not trying to convince you

00:10:56.210 --> 00:10:57.385
one way or another.

00:10:57.385 --> 00:11:01.617
I'm just trying to have an
honest conversation and be funny.

00:11:01.617 --> 00:11:04.222
(laughs) you know, it's
just that that's really it.

00:11:04.222 --> 00:11:08.577
Because I don't want to
step into a pulpit you know,

00:11:08.577 --> 00:11:11.858
a lot of art is starting
to reflect that and

00:11:11.858 --> 00:11:15.238
it's more aspirational than real
and I don't want to be that.

00:11:15.238 --> 00:11:18.090
I don't have a cause that I'm championing.

00:11:18.090 --> 00:11:20.109
I don't have a, and we're going to do, no,

00:11:20.109 --> 00:11:23.359
I just want a funny show
that's not lying to you.

00:11:23.359 --> 00:11:25.730
- [Dan] Where do you get your thoughts,

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:27.089
inspiration when you do stand-up?

00:11:27.089 --> 00:11:28.202
- Uh ...

00:11:28.202 --> 00:11:30.186
- Like, how do you mine
that and not kind of

00:11:30.186 --> 00:11:33.350
cheat on The Carmichael Show?

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:35.986
- They all kind of come together
cause like a lot of times

00:11:35.986 --> 00:11:39.612
if you look in my stand-up
a lot of times you'll hear a

00:11:39.612 --> 00:11:41.499
lot of those things in the show.

00:11:41.499 --> 00:11:44.056
Like it just kind of bleeds
into, cause it's all perspective

00:11:44.056 --> 00:11:46.362
so as long as I feel
strongly about something,

00:11:46.362 --> 00:11:49.838
I can use it for whatever I'm working on.

00:11:49.838 --> 00:11:52.316
With stand-up, a lot of
times, it's the thought that

00:11:52.316 --> 00:11:54.193
I'm running from the most.

00:11:54.193 --> 00:11:56.623
Like the thing that I'm
a little nervous about,

00:11:56.623 --> 00:11:59.434
a little hesitant about and
those are the things that

00:11:59.434 --> 00:12:01.905
that excite me and are usually the most

00:12:01.905 --> 00:12:03.734
fulfilling things to do on stage.

00:12:03.734 --> 00:12:06.317
- [Dan] What makes you nervous?

00:12:07.557 --> 00:12:09.631
- Nothing really. It's not
that it makes me nervous

00:12:09.631 --> 00:12:14.091
as much as that I know that
it's a tightrope. You know?

00:12:14.091 --> 00:12:16.207
Like just to get this thought out it's

00:12:16.207 --> 00:12:18.709
going to kind of be of difficult. (laughs)

00:12:18.709 --> 00:12:21.262
you know those are the things
that it gets me excited.

00:12:21.262 --> 00:12:24.416
I'm never really nervous but it's like the

00:12:24.416 --> 00:12:27.053
things that are going
to be hard to repeat is.

00:12:27.053 --> 00:12:28.300
I'm trying to make it hard for you to

00:12:28.300 --> 00:12:30.534
come back here and repeat later.

00:12:30.534 --> 00:12:31.378
- Bill Cosby.

00:12:31.378 --> 00:12:33.316
(laughter)

00:12:33.316 --> 00:12:34.714
- Well, you all are already very excited.

00:12:34.714 --> 00:12:36.445
(laughter)

00:12:36.445 --> 00:12:40.362
- I want to start by saying
that I met Bill Cosby

00:12:41.864 --> 00:12:44.899
and he was very nice to me.

00:12:44.899 --> 00:12:47.271
(laughter)

00:12:47.271 --> 00:12:48.104
- To me.

00:12:49.496 --> 00:12:52.518
Obviously I can't speak for about 55 women

00:12:52.518 --> 00:12:55.479
who may have had a less
than enjoyable experience

00:12:55.479 --> 00:12:56.472
(laughter)

00:12:56.472 --> 00:12:57.682
- With the Cos.

00:12:57.682 --> 00:12:58.725
(laughter)

00:12:58.725 --> 00:13:00.852
- But he was very nice to me.

00:13:00.852 --> 00:13:02.592
(laughter)

00:13:02.592 --> 00:13:04.058
- I went to his house.

00:13:04.058 --> 00:13:04.891
I did.

00:13:05.817 --> 00:13:07.086
We had tea.

00:13:07.086 --> 00:13:09.678
(laughter)

00:13:09.678 --> 00:13:11.542
I don't remember much after the tea.

00:13:11.542 --> 00:13:14.209
(loud laughter)

00:13:15.502 --> 00:13:16.335
- I'm kidding, obviously,

00:13:16.335 --> 00:13:17.758
I was not drugged and raped by Bill Cosby.

00:13:17.758 --> 00:13:20.303
But we, I don't know, I emailed him.

00:13:20.303 --> 00:13:23.642
I emailed him. I wanted to reach out

00:13:23.642 --> 00:13:25.630
cause you guys are being so mean to him.

00:13:25.630 --> 00:13:27.483
(laughter)

00:13:27.483 --> 00:13:29.674
- Didn't write anything
in the body of the email.

00:13:29.674 --> 00:13:30.635
(laughter)

00:13:30.635 --> 00:13:32.754
- I only wrote in the subject line.

00:13:32.754 --> 00:13:35.005
I did. You want to know
what I wrote? Uh-huh.

00:13:35.005 --> 00:13:35.838
- [Woman] Yeah.

00:13:35.838 --> 00:13:37.936
- I wrote "Hey, my nigga, you good?"

00:13:37.936 --> 00:13:38.980
That's what I wrote to Bill Cosby.

00:13:38.980 --> 00:13:39.813
(loud laughter)

00:13:39.813 --> 00:13:40.760
- I don't know if he's going to respond.

00:13:40.760 --> 00:13:42.417
I don't know if he checks his email.

00:13:42.417 --> 00:13:44.299
(laughter)

00:13:44.299 --> 00:13:45.372
- If he does, he'll ...

00:13:45.372 --> 00:13:47.622
(laughter)

00:13:50.801 --> 00:13:54.335
- Can we talk about how
difficult it is for me?

00:13:54.335 --> 00:13:56.887
This whole Bill Cosby thing.
A lot of you may not relate.

00:13:56.887 --> 00:13:58.329
Maybe he wasn't your favorite comic.

00:13:58.329 --> 00:14:00.496
Maybe you don't do comedy.

00:14:01.426 --> 00:14:03.793
But for me he was a, you know,

00:14:03.793 --> 00:14:07.416
an important person in my life. You know?

00:14:07.416 --> 00:14:11.583
An inspiration if you will
and it's hard. You know.

00:14:11.583 --> 00:14:13.712
It's hard. I'm, you know
it's not that I'm, I'm not

00:14:13.712 --> 00:14:15.636
making any excuses at all. You know.

00:14:15.636 --> 00:14:17.547
Like would I let him give
my mom headache medication?

00:14:17.547 --> 00:14:18.380
Of course not.

00:14:18.380 --> 00:14:20.360
(laughter)

00:14:20.360 --> 00:14:24.685
- But, it's hard watching
your hero fall from grace.

00:14:24.685 --> 00:14:26.435
It's hard. Really is.

00:14:27.427 --> 00:14:29.850
I mean for me, this whole
Bill Cosby thing is like

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:34.214
watching Superman rescue a
boy from a burning building

00:14:34.214 --> 00:14:36.498
and then immediately molest that boy.

00:14:36.498 --> 00:14:38.748
(laughter)

00:14:39.879 --> 00:14:41.142
- You know, leaving me to emotionally

00:14:41.142 --> 00:14:42.348
weigh the good with the bad.

00:14:42.348 --> 00:14:44.598
(laughter)

00:14:47.933 --> 00:14:49.431
- I want to see the good in him.

00:14:49.431 --> 00:14:51.596
I want to see the good
in everybody. You know.

00:14:51.596 --> 00:14:54.348
I try and take what I call
the best friend perspective.

00:14:54.348 --> 00:14:57.490
You know. Where even if someone
did something evil or wrong,

00:14:57.490 --> 00:15:00.716
I try and see the good in em. You know.

00:15:00.716 --> 00:15:02.105
Like a best friend.

00:15:02.105 --> 00:15:03.690
(laughter)

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:05.500
- Like even at Hitler's funeral.

00:15:05.500 --> 00:15:07.229
(laughter)

00:15:07.229 --> 00:15:08.566
- There was still some dude

00:15:08.566 --> 00:15:09.628
who had to get up there and speak.

00:15:09.628 --> 00:15:11.878
(laughter)

00:15:14.057 --> 00:15:15.428
- And it was hard for him.

00:15:15.428 --> 00:15:16.467
(laugher)

00:15:16.467 --> 00:15:19.199
- Society just hating Hitler.

00:15:19.199 --> 00:15:20.978
He knows that Hitler was his best friend

00:15:20.978 --> 00:15:22.825
and he tried to find nice things to say.

00:15:22.825 --> 00:15:24.422
(laughter)

00:15:24.422 --> 00:15:28.965
- Stand up there with his
speech over the coffin.

00:15:28.965 --> 00:15:30.298
Angry reporters.

00:15:32.035 --> 00:15:32.868
Look,

00:15:35.226 --> 00:15:37.364
what can you say about the guy, you know.

00:15:37.364 --> 00:15:39.614
(laughter)

00:15:41.407 --> 00:15:43.402
- I know a lot of you hated him. I get it.

00:15:43.402 --> 00:15:45.089
(laughter)

00:15:45.089 --> 00:15:46.782
- But you don't understand, like

00:15:46.782 --> 00:15:48.636
he helped pay for my
daughter's braces man.

00:15:48.636 --> 00:15:49.469
He was a good dude.

00:15:49.469 --> 00:15:50.302
(laughter)

00:15:50.302 --> 00:15:51.680
- He was a good dude. Okay.

00:15:51.680 --> 00:15:54.878
I know the whole Holocaust thing but

00:15:54.878 --> 00:15:57.255
he used to make these quesadillas, man.

00:15:57.255 --> 00:15:59.320
(loud laughter)

00:15:59.320 --> 00:16:01.597
- And trust me, you
haven't had a quesadilla

00:16:01.597 --> 00:16:03.050
till you had a Hitler quesadilla.

00:16:03.050 --> 00:16:03.883
(laughter)

00:16:03.883 --> 00:16:05.227
- It was something (mumbles)

00:16:05.227 --> 00:16:07.455
(swoosh)

00:16:07.455 --> 00:16:08.941
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:16:08.941 --> 00:16:11.787
I made this video because
Malik Jordan asked me to.

00:16:11.787 --> 00:16:13.090
If there's someone
you'd like me to profile

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:15.736
in a future Top 10, please
check out the link down in

00:16:15.736 --> 00:16:17.865
the description below and
you can cast your vote

00:16:17.865 --> 00:16:19.970
on who we should do next.

00:16:19.970 --> 00:16:22.232
I also want to give a quick
shout out to Bhekisisa.

00:16:22.232 --> 00:16:24.656
Thank you so much for
picking up a copy of my book,

00:16:24.656 --> 00:16:27.419
"Your One Word" and posting
to Twitter about it as well.

00:16:27.419 --> 00:16:29.257
I really really really
appreciate the support

00:16:29.257 --> 00:16:31.256
and I'm so glad that you enjoyed the read.

00:16:31.256 --> 00:16:32.633
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:16:32.633 --> 00:16:33.931
I believe in you.

00:16:33.931 --> 00:16:35.726
I hope you continue to believe in yourself

00:16:35.726 --> 00:16:37.908
and whatever your one word is.

00:16:37.908 --> 00:16:40.062
Much love. I'll see you soon.

00:16:40.062 --> 00:16:42.145
(swoosh)

00:16:46.230 --> 00:16:48.857
- Surround yourself
with like-minded people.

00:16:48.857 --> 00:16:50.357
All my friends ...

00:16:51.332 --> 00:16:53.141
The thing that I feel luckiest with

00:16:53.141 --> 00:16:55.033
is like the group of
people that I'm around.

00:16:55.033 --> 00:16:58.332
I really have excellent friends

00:16:58.332 --> 00:17:02.644
who, like care about like
making things and creating

00:17:02.644 --> 00:17:05.334
and like don't get caught up in

00:17:05.334 --> 00:17:08.089
trivial things that won't matter tomorrow.

00:17:08.089 --> 00:17:09.329
Like we don't get caught up in things

00:17:09.329 --> 00:17:10.362
that don't matter tomorrow.

00:17:10.362 --> 00:17:12.627
Don't get me wrong, we
like new experiences and

00:17:12.627 --> 00:17:16.444
we like having fun but we like work ...

00:17:16.444 --> 00:17:19.527
I was at my office with friends until

00:17:21.452 --> 00:17:23.114
six this morning.

00:17:23.114 --> 00:17:26.528
Five or six this morning
just like, just ... and it

00:17:26.528 --> 00:17:28.272
(crosstalk)

00:17:28.272 --> 00:17:29.652
- [Interviewer] In this context.

00:17:29.652 --> 00:17:31.063
- [Jerrod] Yeah, on Saturday night.

00:17:31.063 --> 00:17:33.637
My entire Saturday, I don't
go out (snorts) really,

00:17:33.637 --> 00:17:35.734
like, you know, do too much.

00:17:35.734 --> 00:17:38.544
Like you know every now and
then but for the most part,

00:17:38.544 --> 00:17:40.417
like just work and my
friends think like that.

00:17:40.417 --> 00:17:43.522
- Do you find it difficult
to tell some of these stories

00:17:43.522 --> 00:17:46.884
that you want to tell in the
allotted time you're given?

00:17:46.884 --> 00:17:49.221
- I mean there's always like a,

00:17:49.221 --> 00:17:52.500
I'm not sure which mic I'm
using here. Boom. There we go.

00:17:52.500 --> 00:17:55.379
There's always a thing or
two that falls out, you know

00:17:55.379 --> 00:17:58.340
that you're excited about,
that you, ah you really wish

00:17:58.340 --> 00:18:02.556
this point or joke or
whatever could make it in but.

00:18:02.556 --> 00:18:06.353
The, what, 21, 50, whatever
the amount that we get is

00:18:06.353 --> 00:18:07.535
- [Female] 21.

00:18:07.535 --> 00:18:08.368
- been okay.

00:18:08.368 --> 00:18:09.201
- [Female] 21, 45.

00:18:09.201 --> 00:18:10.912
- 21, 45, yeah, somewhere around there.

00:18:10.912 --> 00:18:12.259
- [Female] Something small.

00:18:12.259 --> 00:18:13.450
- Something small.

00:18:13.450 --> 00:18:16.053
But we, I'm happy with it

00:18:16.053 --> 00:18:19.565
It's a fun obstacle to
try and write around that.

00:18:19.565 --> 00:18:21.522
Try and capture everything you want to say

00:18:21.522 --> 00:18:22.897
in that amount of time.

